  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that ${5}^{y}={4}^{y+1},$ what is the value of $y?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{y}={4}^{y+1} \\\\ {log}_{5}\u2061({5}^{y}) & ={log}_{5}\u2061({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}\u2061({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}\u2061({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}\u20614\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}\u20614 \\\\ y & =y{log}_{5}\u20614+{log}_{5}\u20614 \\\\ y-y{log}_{5}\u20614 & ={log}_{5}\u20614 \\\\ y(1-{log}_{5}\u20614) & ={log}_{5}\u20614 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{5}\u20614}{1-{log}_{5}\u20614}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}\u2061(4)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)}\u22480.861\\,353.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, we can approximate $y$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\u2248\\frac{0.861\\,353}{1-0.861\\,353}\u22486.213,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places.",
